notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T17:59:48.779Z","harness:claude-code","Reachability and depth baseline measured 2026-07-25 over all 2,027 items, for the bounded-hop invariant this item declares.\n\nActive set (255 items), semantic edges only, breadth-first from the six outcome milestones:\n- unreachable: 0\n- depth histogram: 6 at depth 0 (the milestones), 85 at 1, 98 at 2, 25 at 3, 8 at 4, 10 at 5, 23 at 6\n- maximum semantic depth: 6\n\nIncluding hierarchy edges the maximum falls to 4, which is the expected result and worth stating explicitly: the parent tree is a shortcut, so any invariant measured over all edges will look healthier than the project actually is. The semantic-only measurement is the honest one and should be what this item gates on.\n\nTwo observations for the invariant's design:\n\n1. A third of the active set (85 of 255) sits at semantic depth 1 — attached directly to an outcome milestone. That is a legacy of earlier enrichment passes that optimised for eliminating unreachable items, and direct attachment was the cheapest way to do it. It satisfies a reachability invariant while conveying little: an edge from a Semgrep finding straight to \"provable history\" explains nothing about the path between them. Recommend this item's acceptance criteria include a *minimum* explanatory path quality, not only a maximum hop count — for instance that the path from an execution item to its outcome traverses its capability epic rather than bypassing it.\n\n2. Edge-kind distribution across the whole graph is heavily skewed toward the generic: related 2,007, parent 1,852, blocked_by 364, blocks 69, discovered_from 55, verifies 25, implements 15, child 15, incident_from 1. The typed kinds that carry real planning semantics — implements and verifies — account for 40 of 4,403 edges, under one percent. A bounded-hop invariant computed over `related` edges is close to a connectivity check; computed over typed edges it is a genuine claim about how work reaches an outcome. Recommend the invariant declare which kinds count as explanatory and measure only those.\n\nActive-graph degree is saturated: exactly 28 active items carry a semantic degree of one or less, and all 28 are Semgrep findings whose deliberate hub topology routes through per-file and per-rule representatives. No honest edge remains to be added there, and none should be fabricated."
body: "## Problem\nThe active graph reports zero isolates and zero degree-one nodes, which reads as healthy. It is not the property that matters. What matters for an agent is whether a traversal from an item reaches an outcome that explains it, and how many typed hops that takes. A workspace can be fully connected and still be a flat list wearing a graph costume.\n\n## What good looks like\nFrom any active item, following typed edges reaches an outcome milestone; the path passes through the epic that owns the capability, the feature that delivers it, and the decision that authorised it; and each hop is explainable from the edge kind alone. That is depth, and depth is what makes graph algorithms useful for context assembly rather than decorative.\n\n## Measures to add\nDepth distribution and eccentricity per active node; shortest typed path to any outcome milestone; edge-kind diversity along that path; count of nodes reachable only through a single parent edge; component structure of the active subgraph excluding parent edges, which is the honest test of semantic linkage.\n\n## Boundary\nNo density targets and no fabricated edges. An unreachable item is a signal that either an anchor is missing or the item should not be active — both are answers, and both are better than an invented edge."
